(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How are seborrheic keratoses diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Clinically, with supportive management; cryotherapy may help if itchy or inflamed, though they recur","By biopsy in every case, with excision because they are easily mistaken for neoplasms","Clinically, with excision and negative margins required for every symptomatic lesion","Clinically, with topical fluorouracil applied for two to four weeks as field therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — no treatment is required for asymptomatic lesions. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man treated for scabies has scrubbed his skin with a strong antibacterial soap several times a day and now has widespread irritation. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Vigorous washing is essential to remove the mites from the surface of the skin","The irritation confirms the treatment has failed and a second agent is needed","The irritation indicates a secondary bacterial infection needing oral antibiotics","Excessive washing with harsh soap can worsen the skin irritation and should be stopped"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the permethrin course itself is what treats the infestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"In which patients is Kaposi sarcoma immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome especially dangerous?","choices":["Those with classic Kaposi sarcoma, which is indolent","Those with only oral lesions","Those over seventy years old","Those with visceral disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — endemic or visceral disease and this syndrome can be aggressive and rapidly fatal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with alcohol use disorder has severe pain in the thigh that is far worse than the modest erythema on examination. He was diagnosed with cellulitis two days ago and is no better on oral antibiotics. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Deep vein thrombosis of the femoral vein","Compartment syndrome from an unnoticed injury","Necrotizing fasciitis","Progressing cellulitis needing intravenous therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — pain out of proportion to examination, and no response to antibiotics at forty-eight hours. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with newly diagnosed vitiligo is having her workup arranged. Which investigations are appropriate?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation of a skin scraping taken from the affected patches","Wood's lamp examination in a dark room, with a complete blood count and antinuclear antibody","Punch biopsy of the leading edge sent for direct immunofluorescence and antigen mapping","Minimal erythema dose testing to ultraviolet A and to ultraviolet B before treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the labs address the autoimmune diseases associated with vitiligo. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"The parents ask what the lesion will do as their son grows. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["It will stay exactly the same size and colour for life","It will grow with him, darken and thicken, and will not involute","It will proliferate for a few months and then slowly involute","It will fade within his first year of life"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the vasculature dilates over time and the lesion may become a raised plaque. Psychosocial disability from facial disfigurement can be overwhelming. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What are the first-line treatments for an infantile hemangioma?","choices":["Beta-blockers and corticosteroids; both are named first line","Intralesional corticosteroid alone, with fluorouracil added if the lesion continues to enlarge","Surgical excision in every case, performed before the proliferative phase has finished","Cryotherapy with liquid nitrogen, repeated until the lesion has flattened completely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — propranolol by mouth or timolol topically, whose mechanism is not well understood, and corticosteroids given topically, intralesionally or orally to slow growth and shrink the lesion. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has patchy hair loss with an unusual scarred appearance in one area and diffuse atypical loss elsewhere, and the picture remains unclear after dermoscopy. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin topical minoxidil","Repeat dermoscopy in six months","Scalp biopsy","Begin intralesional corticosteroids empirically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — biopsy is performed when scarring alopecia, diffuse atypical loss or persistent diagnostic uncertainty remains after clinical and dermoscopic assessment. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 139"},{"q":"What does the deck say about school exclusion in tinea capitis?","choices":["Generally unnecessary once effective therapy has begun","The child must be excluded until fungal culture is negative","The child must be excluded for the full duration of oral therapy","Exclusion is required only if a kerion is present"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — follow local policy. And complete the oral course even when itching or scaling improves early. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the natural course of hot tub folliculitis?","choices":["Resolving only after the affected follicles are drained","Usually self-limiting, clearing in two to ten days","Progressive without oral ciprofloxacin in every patient","Chronic and relapsing for months after a single exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ciprofloxacin is reserved for widespread or resistant cases. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient's intertriginous rash has become malodorous with erosions and drainage. Which does this raise concern for?","choices":["Bacterial coinfection","An id reaction at a distant site","Tinea incognito from prior steroid use","Malignant transformation of the plaque"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — malodor, erosion or drainage point beyond Candida. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Nail clipping with periodic acid-Schiff stain evaluates which suspected condition?","choices":["Alopecia areata","Onychomycosis","Psoriatic nail disease","Subungual melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which tests may be used in acute paronychia and what does each exclude?","choices":["Nasal swab for staphylococcal carriage, plucked hair for dermatophytes, biopsy for follicular disease","Gram stain and culture for the bacterial cause, potassium hydroxide for candida, Tzanck smear for herpetic whitlow","Wood's lamp for coral-red fluorescence, potassium hydroxide for dermatophytes, biopsy for malignancy","Blood cultures for bacteraemia, creatine phosphokinase for muscle damage, imaging for osteomyelitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is usually a clinical diagnosis, so testing is directed rather than routine. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"What must the pathologist distinguish when an actinic keratosis is biopsied, and why does it matter?","choices":["Hypertrophic versus atrophic actinic keratosis, because that guides field therapy","Whether the lesion sits on a field-cancerized site, because that guides how often the patient has to be brought back for surveillance review","Actinic keratosis versus squamous cell carcinoma in situ versus invasive squamous cell carcinoma, because invasion changes treatment, margins and risk substantially","Actinic keratosis versus seborrhoeic keratosis, because the treatments differ"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — three possibilities, not two, and the consequence is what makes it worth asking. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has plaques on both elbows that have been present for two years. The clinical picture is not clear-cut and treatment has failed. Which test would settle the diagnosis?","choices":["Direct immunofluorescence","Skin biopsy","Potassium hydroxide preparation","Serum HLA-B27"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — psoriasis is generally a clinical diagnosis, though biopsy may be needed for definitive diagnosis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 162"},{"q":"When is surgical treatment appropriate in vitiligo, and what else is named in management?","choices":["Tissue or cellular grafting only for highly stable disease, alongside psychological intervention and cosmetic therapies","Grafting at first presentation in every patient, since earlier surgery gives a better repigmentation result","Grafting whenever phototherapy has failed after five weeks of narrow band ultraviolet B","Grafting is never used, since the depigmented areas repigment spontaneously over time"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — combining topical therapy with phototherapy is described as ideal. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the only reliable way to diagnose a keratoacanthoma?","choices":["Biopsy","Dermoscopy","Clinical appearance alone","Response to a trial of cryotherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the clinical differential includes three malignancies. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Mental health challenges or cognitive impairment appear in the lecture under which heading?","choices":["Indications for the naturalistic approach","Elements of the FRAMES strategy","Steps of the clinical reasoning process","Barriers to treatment and medication adherence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They are listed among the barriers, alongside cost, low health literacy, cultural or religious beliefs, fear of side effects, transportation and poor follow-up. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has generalised darkly pigmented lentigines over much of his body. Which additional consideration does this raise?","choices":["An internal malignancy should be considered, and urgent screening arranged","An inherited disorder should be considered, as with LAMB and myxoma syndrome","Neurocutaneous melanosis should be considered, and brain imaging arranged","Dysplastic naevus syndrome should be considered, and every lesion biopsied"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a partial or generalised lentigo is what prompts that question. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which element of FRAMES places the decision to change with the patient?","choices":["Feedback about personal risk","Empathetic style","Promote self-efficacy","Responsibility of the patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. FRAMES includes responsibility of the patient as a distinct element, alongside feedback, advice, a menu of options, an empathetic style and promoting self-efficacy. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"For which specific behaviours does the lecture point to counselling guidelines in Bates' Chapter 7?","choices":["Diet, exercise, and sleep hygiene","Medication timing, appointment keeping, and the sharing of records between providers","Weight control, hydration, and screen time","Unhealthy alcohol use, tobacco smoking cessation, and sexually transmitted infections"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture directs students to specific counselling guidelines for adults on these three. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which preventive measures are advised in hidradenitis suppurativa?","choices":["Avoid heat, cleanse daily with antibacterial soap or benzoyl peroxide wash, lose weight, stop smoking","Avoid all water contact, apply a barrier ointment daily, gain weight and use an occlusive dressing","Shower immediately after any hot tub use and monitor the disinfectant levels in the water","Avoid sharing towels or razors and isolate from school until treatment has been underway"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, along with avoiding constrictive clothing and considering laser hair removal. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What does the deck warn against for benign warts likely to resolve on their own?","choices":["Salicylic acid at any concentration","Excessive freezing or destructive therapy","Any period of observation without treatment","Trimming the surface of the lesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — do not over-treat a self-limiting lesion. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"Which layers does cellulitis involve, and which organisms cause it?","choices":["The deeper dermis and subcutaneous tissue, from Group A streptococci or Staphylococcus aureus","The upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, almost always from Group A streptococci","The superficial epidermis alone, from Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes","The stratum corneum of intertriginous skin, from Corynebacterium minutissimum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It may be purulent or non-purulent, which changes the antibiotic choice. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"What should the history cover in a patient with spider angiomas?","choices":["Pregnancies, hormone use, alcohol history, and medications that carry a risk of liver damage","Birth history, feeding and weight gain, and any family history of vascular birthmarks","Footwear, occupation and use of tools or sports equipment","Immobility, incontinence and nutritional status"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesion can be a marker of estrogen excess or liver disease. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A patient has hyperkeratotic tinea pedis with markedly thickened soles. What does the lecture say may help in addition to an antifungal?","choices":["A systemic corticosteroid","An oral antihistamine","A topical antibiotic","A keratolytic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — added to the antifungal for hyperkeratotic disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man treated for erythrasma asks how to stop it returning. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Avoid sharing towels, clothing, bath water, washcloths and razors with anyone else in the household","Stop smoking and avoid constrictive clothing over the affected skin at all times","Use a broad-spectrum sunscreen daily and cover the area with clothing outdoors","Keep the area clean and dry, avoid excessive heat and moisture, and maintain a healthy body weight"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside good general hygiene. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A course of field therapy has cleared a patient's actinic keratoses. What must not be assumed?","choices":["That a nonhealing lesion should be reviewed promptly","That surveillance can stop, because the surrounding field remains at risk","That the treatment reactions of erythema and crusting were abnormal and should not recur","That daily sun protection is still required"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ongoing surveillance remains necessary despite successful therapy. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"In VINDICATE, the letter E stands for which category?","choices":["Eosinophilic","Endocrine (metabolic)","Environmental exposure","Embolic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. E closes the mnemonic as Endocrine, with metabolic given in parentheses. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which long-term measures are offered after a sting-induced anaphylactic reaction?","choices":["Carry oral doxycycline, to be started at the first sign of any systemic symptoms","Carry an epinephrine auto-injector, and desensitisation therapy where the skin test is positive","Carry oral antihistamines, with a short course of prednisolone kept for emergencies","Carry a topical corticosteroid, with local anaesthetic injection for pain control"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — immunotherapy is specified for fire ant hypersensitivity. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has recurrent tapioca-like vesicles on his palms confirmed clinically as dyshidrotic eczema. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["A low-potency topical corticosteroid","A topical antifungal","Oral prednisone tapered over three weeks","A high-potency topical corticosteroid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High potency is specified first line here, with systemic corticosteroids reserved for severe cases. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which populations and regions are particularly affected by cercarial dermatitis?","choices":["The Great Lakes region, and paddy workers and rice farmers of the Far East","The American Midwest and Southeast, particularly people using attics and storage areas","The Pacific Northwest, particularly people working in basements and around wood piles","The southeastern and south central states, particularly rural dwellers in spring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — exposure to cercaria-infested water is what all of them share. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a melanoma in situ confirmed on his forearm. Which re-excision margin does the deck give?","choices":["1 to 2 cm","A uniform 1 cm regardless of thickness","3 to 5 cm","0.5 to 1 cm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — margins widen with Breslow thickness from there. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl with acanthosis nigricans is embarrassed and has been scrubbing her neck daily, believing it is dirt. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The change indicates an underlying cancer in most adolescent patients","The change is a metabolic warning sign rather than a hygiene problem, and 5 to 10% weight loss improves it","The change is caused by friction from clothing against the neck and will settle with softer, looser fabrics","The change is permanent and no intervention will alter its appearance at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the hygiene misconception is specifically addressed in the teaching. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What do multiple Spitz naevi raise the possibility of?","choices":["Dysplastic naevus syndrome","An underlying gastrointestinal malignancy","A familial cancer syndrome","Neurocutaneous melanosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a single lesion carries no such implication. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has a firm 8 mm brown nodule on her lower leg that retracts beneath the skin when compressed from the sides. She is not bothered by it but wants to know her options. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Cryotherapy is the treatment of choice for pigmented dermal nodules","No treatment is needed; a shave or punch biopsy would remove it","Wide local excision is required in every case because of melanoma risk","Intralesional corticosteroid will flatten it over several months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — in a small lesion that biopsy is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Larger lesions may need surgical excision instead. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What mechanism underlies the most common form of acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["A delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction to a contacted allergen","Autoantibody deposition along the epithelial basement membrane zone","Hyperinsulinaemia stimulating keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation through insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor cross-activation","A paraneoplastic process driven by an underlying gastric adenocarcinoma secreting growth factors that act on the epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the insulin-resistant or benign form is the commonest. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl has early Lyme disease with erythema migrans. Which is the most appropriate oral antibiotic?","choices":["Azithromycin","Ceftriaxone","Amoxicillin","Doxycycline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is the alternative first line for early disease in children and pregnant patients. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"In what circumstance do hypertrophic scars characteristically occur?","choices":["When a wound is left to heal by secondary intention rather than closed","When the patient has dark skin, which is strongly associated with them","When a wound becomes infected during the proliferative phase of healing","When scars cross joints or skin creases at a right angle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and they develop soon after surgery rather than months later. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How are severe or extensive allergic contact dermatitis eruptions treated?","choices":["High-dose oral corticosteroids tapered over two to three weeks, because early discontinuation causes high rates of rebound","High-potency topical steroids alone regardless of how extensive the eruption is across the body surface area","A five-day course of oral corticosteroids without taper","Oral antihistamines alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Limited eruptions can be managed with high-potency topical steroids. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman who hikes in an endemic area asks how to protect herself. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Take prophylactic doxycycline through the summer months while she continues to hike","Rely on showering promptly after each hike, since this removes ticks before they attach","Avoid tick habitat, use DEET, PMD or picaridin reapplied about every two hours, and treat clothing with pyrethrins","Request the human Lyme vaccine, which is recommended for anyone living in or visiting an endemic region"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — there is no human vaccine, though one exists for dogs. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 62-year-old has a wart-like lesion on the dorsal hand that has been growing, bleeding, and has not responded to salicylic acid. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Biopsy or refer, since bleeding, growing and refractory lesions warrant it","Repeat the same salicylic acid course for a further twelve weeks","Add a topical corticosteroid to reduce the bleeding and inflammation","Reassure the patient, since bleeding is expected in a resolving wart"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — squamous cell carcinoma is in the differential. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"What causes pityriasis versicolor, and is it contagious?","choices":["Infection with Trichophyton rubrum from shared towels; it is contagious","An autoimmune destruction of melanocytes; it is not contagious","Overgrowth of Malassezia, which normally lives on skin; NOT contagious","Infection with Candida albicans acquired from close contact; it is contagious"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — lipid-dependent Malassezia species. More common with heat, humidity, oily skin, sweating, immunosuppression or corticosteroid exposure. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"In whom is body louse infestation typically found, and how does it persist?","choices":["Schoolchildren sharing hats and hair accessories, persisting through repeated close head contact","Sexually active adults across all social levels, often with a concurrent sexually transmitted infection","Long-term care residents who are elderly and immunosuppressed, persisting through staff carriage","Homeless people, refugees, war victims and those in crowded conditions, persisting via unwashed clothing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — transmission is through contaminated clothing and bedding. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman is recovering from carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome and is being discharged. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Expect complete recovery within weeks, with no need for any specialist follow-up after discharge","Avoid sun exposure and use broad-spectrum sunscreen daily to prevent the eruption from recurring","Avoid the causative drug class for life, wear a medical alert bracelet, and have first-degree relatives counselled on shared genetic risk","Avoid the causative drug for six months, after which a cautious supervised rechallenge in hospital is reasonable to consider"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the human leukocyte antigen association makes family counselling part of the discharge advice. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"How is a macule defined in this lecture?","choices":["A flat elevated plateau lesion measuring greater than one centimetre across","A flat lesion less than one centimetre, without elevation or depression","A flat lesion less than five millimetres","An elevated solid lesion less than one centimetre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A patch is the same lesion larger than one centimetre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Describe the clinical appearance of ephelides and the main differential.","choices":["Beige to black papules and plaques 2 to 20 mm across, with melanoma as the main differential","White non-scaly macules with distinct margins, with tinea versicolor as the main differential","Deep blue macules under 1 cm across, with nodular melanoma as the main differential","Small light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm across, with lentigines as the main differential"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they are asymptomatic and the diagnosis is clinical. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the core mechanism of actinic keratosis, and how does the deck position it relative to keratinocyte carcinoma?","choices":["Human papillomavirus infection of keratinocytes in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is a benign lesion lying on a continuum with verruca rather than with keratinocyte carcinoma","Chronic thermal injury producing dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of scarred skin; it is premalignant and lies on a continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma but is unrelated to ultraviolet exposure","Chronic ultraviolet injury producing dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is premalignant and lies on a biologic continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma, not a distinct separate entity","Chronic ultraviolet injury producing dysplastic melanocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is premalignant but a distinct entity quite separate from keratinocyte carcinoma, with no biologic continuum between them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a continuum, not a separate disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotics are used for furuncles and carbuncles when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is cultured or suspected?","choices":["Penicillin V, erythromycin or clarithromycin","Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin or moxifloxacin","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, clindamycin or doxycycline","Dicloxacillin, cephalexin or amoxicillin-clavulanate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy for the sensitive organism is dicloxacillin or cephalexin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which examination finding separates tinea barbae from bacterial folliculitis?","choices":["The hair is easily removed in tinea barbae","The lesions are painful in tinea barbae and painless in folliculitis","Tinea barbae spares the upper lip","Tinea barbae produces fever and lymphadenopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and bacterial culture can be performed to rule folliculitis out. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How are Stevens-Johnson syndrome, the overlap state and toxic epidermal necrolysis separated?","choices":["By whether the Nikolsky sign is positive or negative at presentation","By body surface area of epidermal detachment: under 10%, 10 to 30%, and over 30%","By the presence of mucosal involvement, which occurs only in the severe form","By whether the trigger was a drug or an infection in each case"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same disease spectrum divided by extent of detachment. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A newborn has a flat brown patch 14 cm across over the lower back, with a slightly uneven border and a pebbly surface. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A café-au-lait macule of the trunk","A dysplastic melanocytic naevus","A large congenital melanocytic naevus","A naevus spilus of the lower back"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — evident at birth or shortly after, and the larger the lesion the higher the melanoma risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"How does polymorphous light eruption typically present?","choices":["An exaggerated sunburn on first exposure to a new medication","Persistent eczematous change in chronically exposed skin of older males","Papules appearing thirty minutes to hours after exposure on the décolletage, forearms and dorsal hands, sparing chronically exposed skin","Painful erythema developing within minutes of ultraviolet exposure and resolving within an hour of moving into the shade"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, resolving in seven to ten days without scarring and recurring each spring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"How is Rocky Mountain spotted fever treated, including in pregnancy and young children?","choices":["Amoxicillin for children and pregnant patients, with doxycycline reserved for adults only","Azithromycin for all patients, since doxycycline is contraindicated below eight years of age","Intravenous ceftriaxone for all patients, with oral therapy reserved for prophylaxis","Doxycycline for adults, pregnant patients and children, with desensitisation where it is contraindicated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 100 mg twice daily for adults and 2.2 mg per kilogram twice daily for children, for 5 to 10 days. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"How broad is the referral requirement in epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Essentially every specialty, given the multisystem burden","Dermatology alone, since the disease is confined to the skin","Genetics alone, since the diagnosis is made on a genetic panel","No routine referral, since the condition is managed in primary care"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — palliative care, genetics and family support are all involved. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What opportunity does the deck say a new lesion in a sun-exposed area creates?","choices":["To counsel on sunscreen, peak-hour sun avoidance and skin checks","To screen for hyperlipidemia and assess cardiac risk","To refer to podiatry if the patient is diabetic","To advise avoiding cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the general education point the deck attaches to every benign lesion in a sun-exposed area. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A patient with a confirmed nail unit melanoma asks whether she will lose the finger. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Amputation is never performed for nail unit melanoma at any depth or extent","Amputation is decided by the number of digits involved rather than by depth or bone involvement","Amputation is not automatic — digit-sparing wide excision or Mohs with immunostaining is contemporary care","Amputation is the automatic first treatment for any nail unit melanoma, whatever the depth or extent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — amputation is reserved for deep, extensive or bone-involving disease, and the digit is preserved where margins can be reliably assessed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 59-year-old has an ivory-white, scar-like plaque on the cheek. He has had no injury there. On careful inspection the pink edge seems to extend further than the visible lesion. Which subtype is this, and what is the concern?","choices":["Superficial basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment","Nodular basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment","Pigmented basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment","Morpheaform or sclerosing basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the invisible extension is what makes it dangerous. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How do urushiol lesions present, and over what time course?","choices":["Well-demarcated plaques shaped like the object contacted, erupting within minutes of exposure and clearing within a day","Diffuse urticarial wheals resolving within 24 hours","Greasy scaling patches in sebum-rich areas over several weeks","Intensely pruritic vesicular lesions in linear distributions, erupting within 4 to 96 hours and persisting up to 3 weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the linear pattern comes from grazing the plant in passing. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What is the hardening phenomenon in polymorphous light eruption?","choices":["Progressive scarring of affected sites over successive seasons","Loss of pigment in the affected areas after each episode","Improvement through the summer as tolerance develops after repeated exposure","Thickening and hardening of the skin from repeated scratching of the eruption"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is also why prophylactic narrowband ultraviolet B in spring is the most effective preventive strategy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 104"}]};
